**How to read the table** <br/>
Each row is an event type, showing how many events happen a year, the average yearly loss in dollars, and that loss as basis points (hundredths of a percent) of assets under management. The last row is the total for all types, and the note under the table shows how bad a single year can get.

**What the table tells you** <br/>
Total expected loss is about $6.0M a year, or 12 bps of assets, inside the 10-15 bps target. Trade errors are most of the events but a small share of the dollars, unauthorized trading is about 1.4 events a year but roughly 40% of the loss, and a bad year (95th percentile) is about 23 bps, so the simulated firm is neither implausibly safe nor implausibly loss-making.

**Sanity check table insights** <br/>
Total expected loss is about $6.0M a year, or 12 basis points of the assumed $5B in assets, which sits within the 10–15 bps target range. Trade errors make up nearly all of the roughly 325 events a year but only a fifth of the loss (2.5 of 12.1 bps), while the 1.4 unauthorized events a year are about 40% of it (4.9 bps), and a bad year (95th percentile) runs to about 23 bps, close to $11.5M.

One note on item 2: the "quarters" in the plot are discovery lags, while the "10-year window" is the horizon of the simulated loss history. If a board member might confuse the two, I'd say "10-year history" in place of "10-year window".

## Scenarios

| Scenario | What changes |
|---|---|
| Baseline | Stable detection, proportional development |
| Heavy-tail shock | Two very large unauthorized-trading losses injected: $60M originating in quarter 12 and discovered in quarter 27 (reported), and $40M originating in quarter 20 and discovered in quarter 42 (after the evaluation date, so undiscovered) |
| Control improvement | **Applies to all three event types.** For every event whose discovery quarter $d$ is after the change quarter $c =$ 24, the discovery quarter becomes $c + \lfloor (d - c)(1 - r) \rfloor$ with $r =$ 50%. This covers both events already pending at $c$ and events that originate afterwards. Severity is drawn before the shift, so only timing changes, not the loss amount. |
| Exposure growth | Volume growth rises to 3% per quarter |

The two example **controls** on the [Control Value](controls.qmd) page work differently from the control-improvement scenario above. They shift the lag-distribution parameters (additively, for the event types listed in `params.yaml`) for the whole simulated history before severities are drawn, so a faster-detected event is also smaller through the $(1 + e \times \text{lag})$ term.
